{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of Interactive Multimedia Learning", "title": "Mayer Principles as Design Constraints", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting mayer principles as design constraints to the Mayer principle-to-edit constraint table with boundary conditions and conflict rankings, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Mayer principle-to-edit constraint table with boundary conditions and conflict rankings shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_foundations_of_interactive_multimedia_learning", "mayer_principles", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "02", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/01/01", "inst-design/03/01"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_calibration"]}, "content": "# Mayer Principles as Design Constraints\n\n![Mayer Principles as Design Constraints textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/01-02-mayer-principles-as-design-constraints.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMayer's principles are usually taught as a checklist of good practices. That framing is a trap. A checklist invites you to apply every item every time, but the principles routinely pull in opposite directions, and several reverse depending on the learner's expertise. This section reframes them as *constraints*: conditional rules that license or forbid a specific design move given a stated learner and goal. The artifact, your diagnosis table, becomes the place where you write which principle governs a given frame, which it overrides, and on what condition. A designer who can name the boundary condition of a principle is doing instructional design; a designer who only recites the principle is decorating.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- State each principle you use as a conditional rule with its boundary condition (learner expertise, modality, pacing).\n- Resolve conflicts between principles by naming which one governs and why.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA tutorial slide arrives for review: a pump diagram, a 120-word paragraph that both narration and on-screen text repeat verbatim, a looping factory video in the background, and a corner legend naming the parts. Three of Mayer's principles fire at once here, and they do not all point the same way. Coherence says delete the video. Redundancy says delete the duplicated text. Spatial contiguity says move the legend onto the parts. The decision is not \"apply Mayer.\" It is \"which principles apply to *this* learner, in what order, and where do they conflict.\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nMayer's twelve principles cluster into three jobs, and each job answers a different design question.\n\n- **Reduce extraneous processing:** coherence (cut the irrelevant), signaling (cue the relevant), redundancy (do not narrate and print the same words), spatial contiguity (put words near their picture), temporal contiguity (sync words and picture in time).\n- **Manage essential processing:** segmenting (let the learner pace chunks), pre-training (teach component names first), modality (offload words to the auditory channel).\n- **Foster generative processing:** multimedia (words plus pictures beat words alone), personalization (conversational voice), voice (human over machine narration).\n\nMatch each principle to the processing job it does, so you can name the job a principle serves before you apply it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Coherence\",\"definition\":\"Reduce extraneous processing by cutting material that serves no instructional objective\"},{\"term\":\"Redundancy\",\"definition\":\"Reduce extraneous processing by not narrating and printing the same words at once\"},{\"term\":\"Spatial contiguity\",\"definition\":\"Reduce extraneous processing by placing words next to the picture part they describe\"},{\"term\":\"Segmenting\",\"definition\":\"Manage essential processing by letting the learner pace the chunks\"},{\"term\":\"Modality\",\"definition\":\"Manage essential processing by offloading words to the auditory channel\"},{\"term\":\"Personalization\",\"definition\":\"Foster generative processing by using a conversational rather than formal voice\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nTwo features make these *constraints* rather than tips. First, **boundary conditions.** The modality and redundancy principles are strongest for novices and for fast-paced, system-controlled material; they weaken or reverse for experts and for learner-paced text (the [expertise-reversal effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expertise_reversal_effect)). Second, **conflict.** On the pump slide, the worked-example impulse to show more text collides with redundancy. You cannot satisfy both by default; you must rank them for the stated learner. Writing the rank, and the condition that justifies it, is the work the table records.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA thin claim hides which principle did the work:\n\n> I made the slide cleaner.\n\nA constraint-grounded claim names the rule and its condition:\n\n> For novice learners on a system-paced slide, the redundancy principle forbids identical narration plus on-screen text, so I deleted the printed paragraph and kept narration. I offered a captions toggle so the redundancy is opt-in for learners who need text, rather than forced on everyone.\n\nThe improvement is that each edit is licensed by a named principle and a stated learner condition, and the exception (the toggle) is justified, not ad hoc.\n\n### Annotated Before/After: One Slide, Three Principles\n\nHere is a single tutorial slide revised by applying Mayer's principles as constraints.\n\n**Before.** A slide shows a pump diagram on the left and, on the right, a 120-word paragraph that both narration and on-screen text repeat word for word. A looping background video of a factory plays behind the slide. Labels for the inlet, piston, and outlet sit in a legend box in the bottom corner.\n\n1. Coherence violation: the background factory video is interesting but extraneous; it competes for visual capacity. Decision: remove the background video.\n2. Redundancy violation: narration and identical on-screen text duplicate the verbal stream for these novice learners. Decision: keep the narration, delete the 120-word printed paragraph.\n3. Spatial-contiguity violation: the inlet, piston, and outlet labels live in a corner legend, away from the parts. Decision: move each of the three labels directly onto the diagram part it names.\n\n**After.** Plain background; pump diagram centered with the three labels placed on the inlet, piston, and outlet; a 22-second narration carries the explanation; no duplicated paragraph.\n\n**Rejected suggestion.** An AI tool proposed adding numbered captions under the diagram restating the narration \"for accessibility.\" Rejected for novices on pedagogical grounds: it reintroduces the redundancy the revision just removed. A captions-on toggle is offered instead so learners who need text can opt in, rather than forcing it on everyone.\n\nThe point is not that the slide is cleaner. Each deletion or move is licensed by a named principle and a stated learner condition.\n\nWalk the pump-slide decisions yourself, choosing which principle governs each conflict for the stated learner.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Resolving the pump slide as constraints\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The learners are novices on a system-paced slide. Narration and an identical 120-word paragraph both deliver the explanation. Which move does the redundancy principle license here?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep both so learners can read and listen\",\"next\":\"keepboth\",\"feedback\":\"For novices on system-paced material, identical narration plus print duplicates the verbal stream and overloads the visual channel.\"},{\"label\":\"Delete the printed paragraph and keep narration\",\"next\":\"deltext\",\"feedback\":\"Correct for this condition: redundancy forbids identical narration plus on-screen text for novices on a system-paced slide.\"}]},\"keepboth\":{\"prompt\":\"The visual channel is now carrying the diagram plus a 120-word paragraph. What is the consequence?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reconsider and remove the duplicated paragraph\",\"next\":\"deltext\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Returning to the redundancy fix relieves the overloaded visual channel.\"}],\"feedback\":\"The split-attention and redundancy costs remain; this is the original problem.\"},\"deltext\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer asks for numbered captions restating the narration for accessibility. What honors both access and redundancy?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add the captions for everyone\",\"next\":\"forced\",\"feedback\":\"That reintroduces the redundancy you just removed and forces duplication on all learners.\"},{\"label\":\"Offer a captions-on toggle so text is opt-in\",\"next\":\"toggle\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: access becomes learner-controlled without forcing duplication on novices who do not need it.\"}]},\"forced\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: how do you give access without recreating redundancy?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Make the captions an opt-in toggle\",\"next\":\"toggle\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. A toggle separates the accessibility axis from the redundancy axis.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Forced duplication undoes the redundancy fix.\"},\"toggle\":{\"prompt\":\"Resolved. Each edit is now licensed by a named principle and the novice, system-paced condition, with access handled by learner-controlled options.\",\"feedback\":\"This is constraint-based design: conditional principles applied only after naming the learner.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTreat each principle as an if-then with a boundary condition, then resolve conflicts explicitly:\n\n1. **Name the condition:** Is the learner a novice or expert? Is the material learner-paced or system-paced? Modality and redundancy effects depend on these.\n2. **Apply the governing principle:** Coherence and contiguity rarely backfire, so apply them first. Modality, redundancy, and segmenting are conditional; apply them only when the condition holds.\n3. **Rank on conflict:** When two principles disagree on a frame, write which governs for this learner and the reason. An unranked conflict is an undecided design.\n\nIf you cannot state the boundary condition, you are using the principle as a slogan, not a constraint.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"more principles applied equals better design.\" Applying personalization, signaling, and segmenting to a frame that only had a redundancy problem adds work without addressing the issue. The second is forgetting reversal: stripping on-screen text for an expert who reads faster than your narrator can speak inserts a new extraneous delay. The third is treating accessibility and redundancy as the same axis; the fix is learner-controlled options (a toggle), not forcing duplication on everyone.\n\n<inline-check question=\"You apply the modality principle (narrate, do not print) to a self-paced reference that expert technicians skim while servicing equipment. Is this the right call?\" explanation=\"Modality and redundancy are strongest for novices on system-paced material. Experts skimming a self-paced reference read faster than narration plays, so forced narration becomes an extraneous delay and the printed text they want is gone. The boundary condition has flipped.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, narration always beats printed text\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, for self-paced experts the condition flips, so printed text they can skim is better\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, because modality is one of the twelve Mayer principles\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"FwSih-S8yOE\" title=\"Mayer's 12 Principles of Multimedia Learning\" caption=\"Use this only after the worked example. Listen for one principle that would force a visible change in your artifact.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk the pump slide through the constraints in order. Coherence is unconditional here: the factory video serves no objective, so it goes, no learner condition needed. Spatial contiguity is also robust: the corner legend forces back-and-forth scanning between word and part, a split-attention cost for any learner, so the labels move onto the parts. Now the conditional principle: redundancy. For these novices on a system-paced slide, identical narration plus print is duplication, so the printed paragraph goes and narration stays. But notice you had to check the condition first; for a self-paced reference where learners scan at their own speed, keeping the text could be the better call. The walkthrough shows the discipline: unconditional principles applied freely, conditional principles applied only after naming the learner, conflicts ranked on the page.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead each principle and immediately ask \"under what condition does this reverse?\" A strong reader never writes a principle without its boundary condition and never resolves a slide without naming which principle governed when two collided. Keep a two-column note: principle on the left, the learner condition that licenses or forbids it on the right.\n\nA strong reader of Mayer's principles as constraints can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which principles fire on this frame, and do any conflict?\n2. What learner condition (expertise, pacing, modality) governs each conditional principle?\n3. When two principles disagree, which governs for this learner, and why?\n4. Which of my edits would reverse if the learner became an expert?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe table is strong when each design edit cites the principle that licensed it and the boundary condition under which that principle holds. \"Removed text\" is weak; \"removed duplicated text per the redundancy principle, valid because these learners are novices on a system-paced slide\" is strong. Treat polish as the last layer. If an edit names no principle, or names a principle with no condition, mark the gap and ask BigAL to compare the same edit under a novice versus an expert learner rather than to restate the principle.\n\nA useful final entry would read: \"I deleted the printed paragraph under redundancy for novices and rejected the AI's caption suggestion because it reintroduced the duplication; the captions toggle preserves access without forcing duplication on everyone.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the slide into a self-paced job aid for experienced technicians who consult it while servicing a pump. The [expertise-reversal effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expertise_reversal_effect) now bites: these readers scan faster than narration plays, so narration becomes the extraneous delay, and the printed text you deleted for novices is exactly what an expert wants to skim. The redundancy principle has not changed; its boundary condition has flipped because the learner and the pacing changed. A designer who wrote \"narration is better\" cannot explain the reversal; a designer who wrote \"modality governs for novices on system-paced material\" can predict it.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Mayer principle-to-edit constraint table packet (each edit cites the governing principle, its boundary condition, and the conflict ranking where two principles collided)](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/01-02-mayer-principles-as-design-constraints-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Mayer principle-to-edit constraint table with boundary conditions and conflict rankings\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"01.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Mayer principle-to-edit constraint table with boundary conditions and conflict rankings.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Mayer principle-to-edit constraint table with boundary conditions and conflict rankings for AIL 606 01.02. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure every edit in your trace names the governing principle, the boundary condition that licenses it, and, where two principles conflicted, which one you ranked first and why.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect Mayer's principles to the revision evidence they license and the bounded role AI support plays in checking conditions.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"mayer_principles\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "mayer_principles", "stem": "A lesson slide places each label and its matching diagram part on opposite sides of the screen, forcing the learner to scan back and forth. Which Mayer principle names the violated constraint, and what is the fix?", "options": ["Coherence principle: delete the diagram entirely.", "Spatial contiguity principle: place each label next to the diagram part it describes.", "Personalization principle: rewrite the labels in conversational style.", "Redundancy principle: read the labels aloud while they stay on the opposite side."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Spatial contiguity says corresponding words and pictures should be placed near each other so the learner does not spend limited capacity scanning to connect them.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_are_absolute", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["mayer_principles"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "mayer_principles", "stem": "A designer narrates a diagram AND prints the identical narration text on screen at the same time, expecting that 'more access' will help novices. Which principle predicts this hurts learning, and why?", "options": ["Signaling principle, because the printed text is a helpful cue.", "Redundancy principle, because identical on-screen text plus narration adds visual load and a reconciliation cost beyond the graphic plus narration alone.", "Segmenting principle, because the narration is too long.", "Multimedia principle, because words should never accompany pictures."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The redundancy principle: for novices, adding on-screen text identical to narration overloads the visual channel. Graphic plus narration alone is generally better.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_redundancy_means_more_is_safer", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["mayer_principles"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "mayer_principles", "stem": "A stakeholder asks you to add a looping background video and motivational music to a procedure tutorial. Treating Mayer principles as design constraints, what is the defensible response?", "options": ["Add both, because engaging media always increases motivation and learning.", "Cite the coherence principle and decline or minimize them, because interesting-but-extraneous material competes for limited capacity and can depress learning.", "Add the music but not the video, since audio never affects the visual channel.", "Add the video but mute it, since silent video is always coherent."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The coherence principle says excluding extraneous material, even when appealing, generally improves learning by protecting capacity for essential and generative processing.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_engaging_extras_help", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["mayer_principles"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_as_design_constraints_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "An expert engineer reports that on-screen text alongside narration actually helps her, contradicting the redundancy principle for novices. What does this boundary condition teach about applying Mayer's principles?", "options": ["The redundancy principle is wrong and should be discarded.", "Principles are constraints with boundary conditions: redundancy effects weaken or reverse for high-knowledge learners and short slow-paced labels, so the designer must specify the learner and conditions before applying a principle.", "All principles apply identically to every learner regardless of prior knowledge.", "Expertise means the learner no longer uses working memory at all."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Mayer's principles carry boundary conditions, including the expertise-reversal effect. A defensible design states the target learner and pacing rather than applying a principle as an absolute rule.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_are_absolute", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "41561f86bce312b515fcc733648b60e12eaf7f8b26bf1865b740c3fbc707904d", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_redundancy_means_more_is_safer", "pattern": "redundancy_helps", "description": "Believing that duplicating narration as identical on-screen text gives learners more access and is therefore safer.", "trigger": "learner adds on-screen text identical to the narration to be thorough", "feedback": "Identical on-screen text plus narration overloads the visual channel and forces reconciliation. For novices, keep the graphic plus narration and drop the duplicated text.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_engaging_extras_help", "pattern": "seductive_details_help", "description": "Assuming that interesting extras such as background video, music, or decorative images raise learning because they raise engagement.", "trigger": "learner adds engaging-but-extraneous media to motivate learners", "feedback": "The coherence principle: extraneous material competes for limited capacity. Remove or minimize seductive details and justify any retained media by the learning goal.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_2_mayer_principles_are_absolute", "pattern": "principles_without_boundary_conditions", "description": "Treating Mayer's principles as universal rules that apply identically to every learner, ignoring expertise reversal and pacing conditions.", "trigger": "learner applies a principle without naming the target learner or conditions", "feedback": "State the principle as a constraint with boundary conditions. Name the target learner, prior knowledge, and pacing before applying it, since some effects reverse for experts.", "rail_action": "represent"}]}